// ServiceGlobalAccessCheck.maxon — A SHARED WORD BELONGS TO ONE GREEN THREAD (E3143), the message-reachability closure.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A MODULE-LEVEL `var` IS ONE WORD, AND A MESSAGE IS A GREEN THREAD THE SCHEDULER MAY PUT ON ANY OS
// THREAD.** Those two facts cannot both hold in a program that compiles, so one of them is refused, and it
// is the ACCESS: ⚖ a message — and anything reachable from one — may neither WRITE nor READ module storage.
// A module-level `let` stays fully legal, and that is the whole of the escape hatch: an immortal,
// written-once word has no second writer for anybody to race.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **NEITHER HALF CAN BE A RUN-TIME CHECK, BECAUSE THERE IS NO MOMENT AT WHICH THE RUNTIME COULD
// NOTICE.** `done = done + 1` is a load, an add and a store; two Ms that interleave those six steps each
// write the other's stale value back. Nothing traps, nothing leaks, no allocation is lost — the program
// simply answers a number that is too small, sometimes. MEASURED at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=16` on the committed
// `sched-runqueue.nothing-is-stolen-on-one-processor`, driven to a fixed total of 1200 sends, ten runs:
// 1200, 1200, **1199**, **1199**, **1198**, **1199**, **1199**, 1200, **1199**, 1200 — **five of ten runs
// lost an update, and every one exited 0.** `specs-shv2/sched-processor.md` records the same class one
// order of magnitude larger. ⇒ the compiler is the only place that can stand, and the ACCESS is the only
// site it can stand at.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE SAME PROGRAM IS PERFECTLY CORRECT AT ONE PROCESSOR**, which is why this rule arrives beside
// the `DefaultMaxProcs` flip and not before it. Every green thread ran on the one M, so the six steps could
// not interleave — which is exactly what several committed cases relied on, in as many words. The refusal is
// what makes that reliance expire at the source instead of in a tally that is occasionally short.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE READ IS REFUSED TOO, AND IT IS THE HALF WITH TEETH — A USE-AFTER-FREE RATHER THAN A LOST
// UPDATE.** ⚖ Ruled after the write rule landed, on this measurement: a global's assignment DECREFS the
// record the slot was holding (`Parser.emitCheckedGlobalStore`), so a reader that has already loaded the
// pointer reads on into freed memory. Twelve services spinning on `label.count()` while `main` ran
// `label = "bravo-{r}"` four thousand times, with an INTERPOLATED value so each store frees a real heap
// record rather than an immortal `.rdata` one:
//
//   | `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=1`  | **8 of 8 clean** |
//   | `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=16` | **20 of 20 exit 139**, several panicking inside `String.count` → `utf8DecodeAt`
//                            with *"Range check failed: value outside typealias 'Codepoint'"* — decoding a
//                            record that had been freed under them |
//
// ⚠ A cheaper probe answers CLEANLY and is worth knowing about, because it is what a first attempt writes: a
// handler that reads the global ONCE, with `main`'s single store either side of it, exits normally every
// time. It never holds the pointer across a store. The hazard needs the reader to be IN the load and the
// writer to be freeing, which is what the spin and the 4,000 stores buy.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RULE IS ABOUT WHO TOUCHES IT, NOT ABOUT GLOBALS AS SUCH.** A write to `self.<field>` is legal — a
// service's fields are reached by ONE green thread, which is the whole point of putting state there. A READ
// of a module-level `let` is legal, and is the escape hatch every configuration constant takes: it is
// written once before any green thread exists and never again, so no store can free anything under a reader.
// And a plain function no message reaches may read and write a module `var` all it likes: a `spawn`
// somewhere does not make the rest of the program concurrent, and the tempting cheap implementation — refuse
// every global access in any program that spawns — inherits the whole-program shape of "is this type a
// service?" where it does not belong.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT OWNS NO CLOSURE OF ITS OWN, AND ITS DIRECTION IS THE OPPOSITE OF EVERY OTHER SUMMARY'S.** The
// four whole-program summaries in `SemanticCheck` and the blocking graph in `ServiceCallCycleCheck` all ask
// which functions are AFFECTED BY a fact, which travels UP into callers (`markSeededCallers`). This one asks
// *"what does a message RUN?"*, which travels DOWN into callees — the same engine, rowed by the other column,
// spelled once in `markSeededCallees`. The seed predicate (`messageOwnerNameOf`) and the edge predicate
// (`opIsACallGraphEdge`) are the cycle check's own, shared rather than restated, so the two rules cannot come
// to disagree about what a message is or about which op is a call.
//
// ⭐ **AND THE WALK IS SHARED TOO, NOW.** The predicates were single-sourced while the SWEEP was not: this
// rule and E3139 each built their own `CallGraphEdges` and their own `ownerPerFunc`, back to back, out of two
// identical passes over every function's every op. `ServiceProgramSurvey` is that pass, written once; both
// rules read the same object, so a future change to "the ops of a function" cannot reach one and not the
// other.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AN UNNAMEABLE DISPATCH REACHED FROM A MESSAGE MAKES EVERY FUNCTION MESSAGE-REACHABLE, AND THAT IS
// THIS FILE'S REFUSING DIRECTION — THE EXACT OPPOSITE OF `ServiceCallCycleCheck`'s.** An `indirectCall`
// through a closure and a `witnessDispatch` both choose their target at run time, so neither contributes an
// edge; the two rules then have to decide what that silence MEANS, and they decide it oppositely because
// they fail oppositely. A missed edge THERE costs a deadlock-freedom guarantee for a program shape nobody
// writes, while a false refusal is unworkaroundable — so it accepts. A missed edge HERE costs a **silently
// wrong answer at run time**, the one failure this project has no instrument for; a false refusal costs an
// author a restructuring the language already prescribes. So it refuses, and it says so in the diagnostic
// rather than pretending it found a call path (`SharedGlobalAccessRoute.throughAnUnnameableDispatch`).
//
// ⭐ **BOTH VERDICTS ARE RUN, ONE INDIRECT CALL APART.**
// `green-thread-globals.error.a-dispatch-the-compiler-cannot-follow-widens-the-rule` and
// `green-thread-globals.a-plain-function-may-write-a-module-global` are the SAME program except that the
// first routes its handler through a closure value; they answer refused and accepted respectively. Delete
// the `indirectCall gives true` arm below and the first goes green, which is the sabotage this paragraph is
// proved by.
//
// ⚠ **AN UNRESOLVABLE *NAMED* CALLEE IS NOT ONE OF THOSE, AND TREATING IT AS ONE WOULD MAKE THE RULE
// VACUOUS-BY-REFUSAL.** A name no `IrFunction` declares is a Std-tier runtime entry point or an extern —
// `__mm_retain`, `__write_stdout`, the `__mf_*` band — installed after lowering and declared in no source at
// all. None of them has a body that could write a user's module slot, and none calls back into user code, so
// following them buys nothing. Counting them would mark every handler that prints or allocates as blind and
// refuse essentially every service program in the corpus.

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A MESSAGE CAN RUN — the named closure, WIDENED WHEN A FUNCTION INSIDE IT DISPATCHES SOMEWHERE
// THE EDGES CANNOT EXPRESS.** See this file's header for why the widening is the safe direction here and the
// wrong one next door, and why it is a single step rather than a fixpoint.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE WIDENING IS TO THE FUNCTIONS A DISPATCH COULD ACTUALLY LAND ON, AND IT USED TO BE TO EVERY
// FUNCTION IN THE PROGRAM — WHICH MADE THE RULE UNUSABLE.** A function value in this language comes from a
// `functionRef` or a witness slot and from nowhere else, so a function that is neither address-taken nor a
// conformance member has no address anywhere in the image and no `indirectCall` or `witnessDispatch` can
// reach it. Marking it anyway was not conservatism, it was a wrong answer in the refusing direction:
// MEASURED, a service whose handler called ONE ordinary interface method and then `Log.trace` was refused
// with *"`Log.startCapture` writes the module-level `capturing` … make `capturing` a `let`"* — a cure in
// `stdlib/Log.maxon`, which the author does not own, and `stdlib/Testing.maxon` has the same shape. Every
// service program using an interface was refused.
//
// ⚠ **THE SAFE DIRECTION IS KEPT WITHIN THE NARROWED SET, and it is still a WIDENING**: the possible targets
// are added to the seeds and the closure then runs FORWARD over the named graph, so everything one of them
// calls is marked too. `possibleIndirectCallTargets` is where the enumeration's completeness is argued.
//
// ⭐ **SABOTAGE-PROVED.** With `possibleIndirectCallTargets` replaced by a column of all-`true` — the old
// behaviour — `green-thread-globals.an-interface-in-a-handlers-cone-does-not-implicate-the-stdlib` goes RED
// with exactly the sentence above (`Log.startCapture` writes `capturing`), and the other seven cases in that
// file, `error.a-dispatch-the-compiler-cannot-follow-widens-the-rule` included, stay green. The narrowing is
// the only thing that case is measuring, and it does not weaken the refusal beside it.
function functionsAMessageCanRun(project Project, funcNames FunctionNameIndex, survey ServiceProgramSurvey) returns BoolArray
	let named = markSeededCallees(survey.edges, seeds: survey.messageSeeds, nodeCount: survey.funcCount())
	if not aReachedFunctionDispatchesBlindly(survey, reached: named) 'everyTargetIsNamed'
		return named
	end 'everyTargetIsNamed'

	var widened = BoolArray.create()
	let targets = survey.possibleIndirectCallTargets(project, funcNames: funcNames)
	for funcIndex in 0 upto survey.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let alreadyNamed = try named.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("functionsAMessageCanRun: named.get OOB at {funcIndex} below count {named.count()}")
		let reachableByADispatch = try targets.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("functionsAMessageCanRun: targets.get OOB at {funcIndex} below count {targets.count()}")
		widened.push(alreadyNamed or reachableByADispatch)
	end 'eachFunc'

	return markSeededCallees(survey.edges, seeds: widened, nodeCount: survey.funcCount())
end 'functionsAMessageCanRun'

// Does any function a message can run choose a callee at run time? Asked of the NAMED-path answer, which is
// why the widening above is one step rather than a fixpoint: the widened seed set is fixed and the closure
// over it settles in one pass.
function aReachedFunctionDispatchesBlindly(survey ServiceProgramSurvey, reached BoolArray) returns bool
	for funcIndex in 0 upto survey.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let messageRunsIt = try reached.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("aReachedFunctionDispatchesBlindly: reached.get OOB at {funcIndex} below count {reached.count()}")
		let blind = try survey.dispatchesBlindly.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("aReachedFunctionDispatchesBlindly: dispatchesBlindly.get OOB at {funcIndex} below count {survey.funcCount()}")
		if messageRunsIt and blind 'aTargetThisWalkCannotName'
			return true
		end 'aTargetThisWalkCannotName'
	end 'eachFunc'

	return false
end 'aReachedFunctionDispatchesBlindly'

// What ONE function can run. Error path only — the widened answer above says a message reaches a run-time
// dispatch without saying whose, and this is what asks each message in turn.
function coneOf(survey ServiceProgramSurvey, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns BoolArray
	return markSeededCallees(survey.edges, seeds: oneHotSeeds(survey, funcIndex: funcIndex), nodeCount: survey.funcCount())
end 'coneOf'

// Who can run ONE function — the same graph rowed the way every other summary in the compiler rows it.
// Error path only, for `blameForSharedGlobalAccess`'s stated reason.
function callersReaching(survey ServiceProgramSurvey, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns BoolArray
	return markSeededCallers(survey.edges, seeds: oneHotSeeds(survey, funcIndex: funcIndex), nodeCount: survey.funcCount())
end 'callersReaching'

// A seed column holding exactly one function. Written once because both error-path closures need one and a
// second spelling would be free to size it differently — which is the single thing the mark walk panics on.
function oneHotSeeds(survey ServiceProgramSurvey, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns BoolArray
	var seeds = BoolArray.create()
	for candidate in 0 upto survey.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		seeds.push(candidate == funcIndex)
	end 'eachFunc'
	return seeds
end 'oneHotSeeds'

// ⭐⭐ **THE EARLIEST ACCESS OF ONE DIRECTION THAT A MESSAGE CAN RUN, REPORTED — OR `false` IF THERE IS
// NONE.** Answering rather than reporting-and-hoping is what lets the caller run it twice, which is the
// whole of the ranking rule below.
function reportEarliest(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcNames FunctionNameIndex, survey ServiceProgramSurvey, reached BoolArray, wanted ModuleVarAccess) returns bool
	for row in 0 upto survey.accessRowCount() 'eachAccess'
		let site = survey.accessSiteAt(row)
		if not sameModuleVarAccess(site.access, other: wanted) 'theOtherDirection'
			continue
		end 'theOtherDirection'

		let funcIndex = survey.accessFuncAt(row)
		if not (try reached.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("reportEarliest: reached.get OOB at {funcIndex} below count {reached.count()}")) 'noMessageRunsIt'
			continue
		end 'noMessageRunsIt'

		reportSharedGlobalAccess(project, signatures: signatures, funcNames: funcNames, survey: survey, funcIndex: funcIndex, site: site)
		return true
	end 'eachAccess'

	return false
end 'reportEarliest'

// A `union` carries no `==`, and these two arms are compared in exactly one place. `TypeCycleCheck`'s rule:
// a two-arm test written as a `match` covers the union exhaustively, so a third direction fails to compile
// here rather than silently reading as "not the one I wanted".
function sameModuleVarAccess(access ModuleVarAccess, other ModuleVarAccess) returns bool
	return match access 'access'
		read gives match other 'other'
			read gives true
			write gives false
		end 'other'
		write gives match other 'other'
			read gives false
			write gives true
		end 'other'
	end 'access'
end 'sameModuleVarAccess'

// ⭐ **THE E3143 CHECK.** Close the message seeds forward over the shared call graph and refuse the first
// write a message can run.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO GATES ARE ASKED BY `checkServiceRules`**, which owns the survey this rule shares with E3139 —
// `moduleVarAccessSites` is empty for every program that never names a module `var` (the parser records a
// site only at `storeToGlobal` and at an author's `emitGlobalLoad`), and the service roster is empty for
// every program that spawns nothing.
//
// A survey with no access ROW is a different answer from an empty site MAP: every recorded access belonged
// to a body dead-code elimination dropped, so there is nothing to close over.
module function checkServiceGlobalAccesses(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcNames FunctionNameIndex, survey ServiceProgramSurvey)
	if survey.accessRowCount() == 0 'everyRecordedAccessBelongsToADroppedBody'
		return
	end 'everyRecordedAccessBelongsToADroppedBody'

	reportFirstAccessAMessageCanRun(project, signatures: signatures, funcNames: funcNames, survey: survey, reached: functionsAMessageCanRun(project, funcNames: funcNames, survey: survey))
end 'checkServiceGlobalAccesses'

// ⭐ **THE FIRST OFFENDING ACCESS, AND ONLY THE FIRST.** One statement is one defect and the cure is the same
// for every other access to the same global; reporting each would bury the one the author has to change
// under its own consequences, exactly as `blameForTypeCycles` refuses to double-diagnose one ring.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A WRITE OUT-RANKS A READ, AND THAT IS NOT A TIE-BREAK — IT IS WHAT KEEPS ONE DEFECT FROM HAVING TWO
// SENTENCES.** `total = total + by` emits the RHS's LOAD before the store, so in op order the READ of `total`
// comes first; `total = 5` has no load at all. Reported in program order, one read-modify-write would be
// diagnosed as *"the message reads the module-level `total`"* anchored at the right-hand occurrence, while
// the identical defect one character shorter would be diagnosed as a write — the same fault, two subjects,
// decided by whether the statement happens to mention its own target. **MEASURED as exactly that**, on this
// file's own three committed write cases, the moment the read half landed.
//
// ⇒ the ranking is by DIRECTION first and position second, and the direction it prefers is the write: a
// read-modify-write IS a write, and a function that writes a shared word is the reason every reader of that
// word is unsafe. Naming the root rather than the consequence is the same call `blameForTypeCycles` makes.
function reportFirstAccessAMessageCanRun(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcNames FunctionNameIndex, survey ServiceProgramSurvey, reached BoolArray)
	if reportEarliest(project, signatures: signatures, funcNames: funcNames, survey: survey, reached: reached, wanted: ModuleVarAccess.write) 'aWriteWasReported'
		return
	end 'aWriteWasReported'

	_ = reportEarliest(project, signatures: signatures, funcNames: funcNames, survey: survey, reached: reached, wanted: ModuleVarAccess.read)
end 'reportFirstAccessAMessageCanRun'

// The diagnostic: anchored at the WRITE, which is where the program can be repaired, naming the message that
// reaches it and carrying the `spawn` that made the type a service as a NOTE — `SERVICES_DESIGN.md`'s
// standing requirement for every whole-program service rule, because the cause may be in another file.
function reportSharedGlobalAccess(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcNames FunctionNameIndex, survey ServiceProgramSurvey, funcIndex FuncIndex, site ModuleVarAccessSite)
	let blame = blameForSharedGlobalAccess(survey, funcIndex: funcIndex)
	let writer = try funcNames.functions.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("reportSharedGlobalAccess: functions.get OOB at {funcIndex}")
	let message = try funcNames.functions.get(blame.messageIndex) otherwise panic("reportSharedGlobalAccess: functions.get OOB at {blame.messageIndex}")
	let serviceName = survey.serviceOf(blame.messageIndex)
	let spawn = serviceSpawnSiteOf(signatures, typeName: serviceName)
	reportPositionedWithNote(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticSharedGlobalAccessFromGreenThread, message: sharedGlobalAccessRefusal(blame.route, access: site.access, writer: writer.name, message: message.name, storageName: site.storageName), line: site.line, column: site.column, filePath: site.declFilePath, note: DiagnosticNote.at(serviceSpawnNoteText(serviceName), line: spawn.line, column: spawn.column, filePath: spawn.declFilePath))
end 'reportSharedGlobalAccess'

// ⭐ **WHICH MESSAGE RUNS THE WRITER, AND BY WHICH ROAD.** The road is part of the answer and not a detail:
// the sentence may not claim a call path when the only thing that reached the writer was the widening, or
// the author goes looking for a call that is not in their program.
type SharedGlobalAccessBlame
	export let messageIndex as FuncIndex
	export let route as SharedGlobalAccessRoute

	static function create(messageIndex FuncIndex, route SharedGlobalAccessRoute) returns SharedGlobalAccessBlame
		return Self{messageIndex: messageIndex, route: route}
	end 'create'
end 'SharedGlobalAccessBlame'

union SharedGlobalAccessRoute
	// The writer IS the message — the direct shape, and the one an author would have spotted.
	itself
	// A chain of NAMED calls runs from the message to the writer.
	throughCalls
	// No named chain does. The message reaches a dispatch whose target is chosen at run time, so this writer
	// is a function that dispatch could land in — the header's refusing direction, said out loud.
	throughAnUnnameableDispatch
end 'SharedGlobalAccessRoute'

// ⭐ **THE SECOND — AND ONLY OTHER — CLOSURE, AND IT RUNS ON THE ERROR PATH ONLY**, on
// `ServiceCallCycleCheck.reportServiceCallCycle`'s discipline: a program that compiles never pays for it.
// The forward closure above proves SOME message reaches this writer without saying which, so the reverse one
// asks the question the other way round — who can reach the writer? — and the earliest message in that set
// is the answer. Earliest by function index, which is declaration order, so it is the one the author wrote
// first rather than whatever a worklist happened to drain first.
function blameForSharedGlobalAccess(survey ServiceProgramSurvey, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns SharedGlobalAccessBlame
	if survey.isAMessage(funcIndex) 'theWriterIsTheMessage'
		return SharedGlobalAccessBlame.create(funcIndex, route: SharedGlobalAccessRoute.itself)
	end 'theWriterIsTheMessage'

	let callers = callersReaching(survey, funcIndex: funcIndex)
	for candidate in 0 upto survey.funcCount() 'eachCaller'
		let reachesTheWriter = try callers.get(candidate) otherwise panic("blameForSharedGlobalAccess: callers.get OOB at {candidate} below count {callers.count()}")
		if reachesTheWriter and survey.isAMessage(candidate) 'aMessageCallsIt'
			return SharedGlobalAccessBlame.create(candidate, route: SharedGlobalAccessRoute.throughCalls)
		end 'aMessageCallsIt'
	end 'eachCaller'

	return SharedGlobalAccessBlame.create(messageReachingAnUnnameableDispatch(survey), route: SharedGlobalAccessRoute.throughAnUnnameableDispatch)
end 'blameForSharedGlobalAccess'

// ⭐ **THE MESSAGE THE WIDENING CAME FROM.** Reached only when no named chain explains the access, which means
// the widening is what marked it — and the widening fires exactly because some message's own cone holds a
// run-time dispatch. One forward closure per MESSAGE, on the error path of a program with one or two
// services; the earliest such message is the one named, for `blameForSharedGlobalAccess`'s ordering reason.
function messageReachingAnUnnameableDispatch(survey ServiceProgramSurvey) returns FuncIndex
	for candidate in 0 upto survey.funcCount() 'eachMessage'
		if not survey.isAMessage(candidate) 'notAMessage'
			continue
		end 'notAMessage'

		if aReachedFunctionDispatchesBlindly(survey, reached: coneOf(survey, funcIndex: candidate)) 'itsConeHoldsOne'
			return candidate
		end 'itsConeHoldsOne'
	end 'eachMessage'

	panic("messageReachingAnUnnameableDispatch: no message's own cone holds a run-time dispatch, yet the widening marked a writer no named chain reaches — the widening fires only from `aReachedFunctionDispatchesBlindly` over the seeds this walk re-closes one message at a time")
end 'messageReachingAnUnnameableDispatch'

// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE SENTENCE: who writes, why one word cannot have two writers, and the cure.** The rule and
// the cure are written ONCE and the ROUTE supplies only the subject clause — three copies of the race
// paragraph would be three chances to describe the same defect differently.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A `String` LITERAL BUILT INSIDE THE FUNCTION**, on `ServiceCallCycleCheck.serviceCallCycleMessage`'s
// finding: a module-level `String` constant seeded into an accumulator is a lazily-initialized static that a
// `var` binding CO-OWNS, and `append` then writes through into the static — MEASURED there as a message that
// accumulated across compiles.
function sharedGlobalAccessRefusal(route SharedGlobalAccessRoute, access ModuleVarAccess, writer ByteArray, message ByteArray, storageName ByteArray) returns String
	let global = String.from(storageName)
	var refusal = sharedGlobalAccessSubject(route, access: access, writer: writer, message: message, global: global)
	refusal.append(" — a message runs on a green thread the scheduler may put on any OS thread, and a module `var` is one word every one of them shares. ")
	refusal.append(sharedGlobalAccessHazard(access, global: global))
	refusal.append(" Keep it in a field of `self` and hand it back through a reply, or make `{global}` a `let`")
	return refusal
end 'sharedGlobalAccessRefusal'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO DIRECTIONS FAIL DIFFERENTLY, AND ONLY THE HAZARD SENTENCE KNOWS IT.** A racing WRITE loses an
// update and answers a number that is too small; a racing READ can be worse, because the write that changes
// the slot RELEASES the record it was holding (`Parser.emitCheckedGlobalStore`) — so the reader is not
// looking at a stale word, it is following a pointer into freed memory. Saying "the program answers a number
// that is too small" about a read would understate a use-after-free, and one sentence covering both would be
// true of neither.
//
// ⚠ **THE READ SENTENCE SAYS "WHEN IT HOLDS A String, AN ARRAY OR A STRUCT", AND THE HEDGE IS LOAD-BEARING
// RATHER THAN CAUTIOUS.** A SCALAR global's slot holds no record and frees nothing; racing it yields a stale
// word and no more. The site this diagnostic is built from carries the slot's NAME and not its TYPE, so the
// sentence states the ladder instead of picking a rung — which is honest for both, where naming a released
// record would be simply FALSE about `var n = 0`.
function sharedGlobalAccessHazard(access ModuleVarAccess, global String) returns String
	return match access 'access'
		read gives "A reader gets whichever side of a concurrent write the scheduler happened to interleave — and when `{global}` holds a String, an array or a struct that is not a stale word but a FREED ONE, because the write RELEASES the record the slot was holding while the reader is still following the pointer it already loaded. Nothing traps at the store."
		write gives "So `{global} = …` is a load, an add and a store that two of them can interleave, each writing the other's stale value back. Nothing traps when they do; the program answers a number that is too small."
	end 'access'
end 'sharedGlobalAccessHazard'

// The subject clause, one arm per road. The direct shape leads with the MESSAGE because that is the whole
// sentence; the two indirect ones lead with the WRITER, because that is the line the caret sits on and the
// reader needs to know why an ordinary function — which may write a global all it likes — is being refused.
function sharedGlobalAccessSubject(route SharedGlobalAccessRoute, access ModuleVarAccess, writer ByteArray, message ByteArray, global String) returns String
	let verb = sharedGlobalAccessVerb(access)
	return match route 'road'
		itself gives "the message `{String.from(message)}` {verb} the module-level `{global}`"
		throughCalls gives "`{String.from(writer)}` {verb} the module-level `{global}`, and the message `{String.from(message)}` reaches it"
		throughAnUnnameableDispatch gives "`{String.from(writer)}` {verb} the module-level `{global}`, and the message `{String.from(message)}` dispatches through a closure or a witness whose target this compiler cannot name, so it may land here"
	end 'road'
end 'sharedGlobalAccessSubject'

// The one word the two directions differ by. Present tense and third person, because every subject clause
// above puts a single actor in front of it.
function sharedGlobalAccessVerb(access ModuleVarAccess) returns String
	return match access 'access'
		read gives "reads"
		write gives "writes"
	end 'access'
end 'sharedGlobalAccessVerb'

// Does this op hand control somewhere no NAME can be recovered for? The bands are `opYields`', so a newly
// appended op fails to compile here rather than silently reading as followable — which is this rule's
// accepting direction and the one it must never take by accident.
module function opChoosesItsTargetAtRunTime(op MaxonOp) returns bool
	return match op 'op'
		call to branch gives false
		globalAddr to tryCall gives false
		throwError to panicAbort gives false
		// Taking a function's ADDRESS runs nothing; the `indirectCall` that eventually runs it is the op below.
		functionRef gives false
		indirectCall gives true
		asyncCall gives false
		await to switch gives false
		witnessDispatch to witnessTryDispatch gives true
		panicAbortValue gives false
		errorFlagEncode gives false
		// A `spawn` names a TYPE and a send names a MESSAGE — both are names, and a send's handler is a seed
		// of this walk in its own right.
		serviceSpawn to serviceReplyCell gives false
	end 'op'
end 'opChoosesItsTargetAtRunTime'
